"""Report text for the FINAL XLM-R vs TF-IDF study (section 4.5 + executive summary + abstract/conclusion lines). Every number is read from experiments/xlmr_vs_tfidf_final/outputs;
the verdict wording is chosen by the pre-registered decision rule, so the text cannot claim more than the statistics support. Called from scripts/make_pbl_report.py."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
FIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent; ROOT = FIN.parents[1]; O = FIN / "outputs"; FIG = FIN / "figures"
J = lambda p: json.loads(Path(p).read_text()) if Path(p).exists() else None
f2 = lambda x: "N/A" if x is None else f"{x:.2f}"; f3 = lambda x: "N/A" if x is None else f"{x:.3f}"; f4 = lambda x: "N/A" if x is None else f"{x:.4f}"; s3 = lambda x: "N/A" if x is None else f"{x:+.3f}"
def ms(x): return "N/A" if x is None else (f"{x:.1f}" if x >= 10 else f"{x:.2f}")
def mb(x): return "N/A" if x is None else (f"{x:.0f}" if x >= 100 else f"{x:.1f}")
def tsec(x): return "N/A" if x is None else (f"{x / 60:.1f} min" if x >= 120 else f"{x:.0f} s")

def load():
    D = dict(S=J(O / "stats/primary256_stats.json"), ph1=J(O / "phase1_reconciliation.json"), tr=J(O / "truncation_audit.json"), cost=J(O / "cost_analysis.json"), om=J(O / "other_models_analysis.json"), pw=J(O / "power_analysis.json"), sel=J(FIN / "configs/selected_variant.json"),
             tf=J(O / "tfidf/config.json"), legacy=J(ROOT / "experiments/text_vs_neural/outputs/results.json"), variants={v: J(O / f"stats/{v}_stats.json") for v in ("len512", "chunk256", "ep10")}, ver=J(FIN / "verification/final_verification.json"),
             ten=J(O / "stats/primary256_seeds0to9_stats.json"), usyn=J(O / "unseen_synthetic/stats.json"), ureal=J(O / "unseen_real/stats.json"), uxs=J(O / "unseen_synthetic/extras.json"), uxr=J(O / "unseen_real/extras.json"), hy=J(O / "hybrid/hybrid_stats.json"))
    return D

def claims(S):
    P = S["primary"]; d = P["delta_macro_f1"]; lo, hi = P["ci95_hierarchical"]
    return dict(observed=d > 0, stat=(d > 0 and lo > 0), prac=lo > 0.02, inferior=hi < 0, d=d, lo=lo, hi=hi, label=P["decision"])

def cost_facts(D):
    c = D["cost"]; r = c.get("ratios_xlmr_over_tfidf", {}); m = c["per_model"]; return c, r, m

def exec_summary(para, bullet, D):
    S, C = D["S"], claims(D["S"]); c, r, m = cost_facts(D); P = S["primary"]; stb = S["stability"]; n = len(S["seeds"]); ra, rs = r.get("cpu_end_to_end"), r.get("artifact_size")
    para("This project builds a working prototype that reads the bills submitted by Indian employees and prepares them for human resources review. Each bill is read by optical character recognition and classified into one of 16 expense categories by a calibrated fusion of three models, and up to 34 fields are extracted from it. A bill that the first model cannot place is passed to a second classifier for twelve tax-evidence document types, such as rent receipts and salary slips. On held-out real documents the first stage reaches 0.907 accuracy and 0.839 macro-F1, with 93.0% of the bills handled automatically at 94.4% precision, and the field extractor reaches 0.663 micro-F1. The second stage was trained on synthetic documents only, so its perfect synthetic scores show that it is integrated correctly but say nothing about its accuracy on real documents.", align="j")
    verdict = ("the interval excludes zero, so the advantage is statistically supported" + (" and exceeds the practical threshold of 0.02" if C["prac"] else " but is not shown to exceed the practical threshold of 0.02")) if C["stat"] else ("the interval includes zero, so the advantage is not established on the 386-document real test set" if not C["inferior"] else "the interval lies below zero, so XLM-R is worse")
    extra = (f" Ten training seeds, two unseen test sets (one synthetic and one small real set) and a hybrid that sends only uncertain documents to XLM-R did not change this conclusion." if D.get("ten") and D.get("usyn") and D.get("ureal") else "")
    para(f"The report also examines whether the large transformer XLM-R should replace the simple TF-IDF text classifier. Over {n} training seeds XLM-R reached a macro-F1 of {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} against {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f} for TF-IDF (a difference of {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f}), but {verdict} (95% interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}).{extra} XLM-R also needs {rs:.0f} times the storage and {ra:.0f} times the processor time per document, so the measured gain does not justify its cost." if ra and rs else "The report also compares XLM-R with the TF-IDF text classifier; resource measurements were not available.", align="j")
    para("TF-IDF with logistic regression therefore remains the recommended text classifier for production. The conclusions are limited by the small number of real test documents in several classes, by the 256-token input limit of XLM-R, and by the absence of real documents for the second stage; the most useful next step is a larger, labelled set of real documents from unseen templates, which would also decide whether XLM-R or the cheaper hybrid is worth deploying.", align="j")

def build_main(para, bullet, table, figure, D):
    S, C = D["S"], claims(D["S"]); ph, tr, c, om, pw = D["ph1"], D["tr"], D["cost"], D["om"], D["pw"]; P = S["primary"]; stb = S["stability"]; n = len(S["seeds"]); cc, r, m = cost_facts(D); lg = D["legacy"]
    para("4.5 XLM-RoBERTa versus TF-IDF: Final Multi-Seed Study", bold=True)
    para("This section settles the two questions left open in 4.4: is a fine-tuned XLM-RoBERTa demonstrably better than TF-IDF + logistic regression, and is any gain worth its cost? The design, the primary metric (macro-F1 on the 386 real test documents), the five training seeds, the decision rule and the 0.02 practical threshold were written down before any new training (experiments/xlmr_vs_tfidf_final/PREREGISTRATION.md); checkpoints were selected on validation data only.", align="j")
    # ---- 4.5.1 reconciliation ----
    para("4.5.1 Reconciled classification metrics", italic=True)
    A, B_ = ph["recomputed"]["tfidf"], ph["recomputed"]["xlmr_original"]; art = ph["artifacts"]
    rows = [["Metric (real test, n = 386)", "TF-IDF + LR", "XLM-R original single run", "Source artifact"]] + [[nm, f4(A[k]), f4(B_[k]), "stored logits recomputed (max abs. diff vs stored metrics < 1e-7)"] for nm, k in (("Accuracy", "accuracy"), ("Macro precision", "macro_precision"), ("Macro recall", "macro_recall"), ("Macro-F1", "macro_f1"), ("Weighted-F1", "weighted_f1"), ("ECE (10 bins, temperature-scaled)", "ece"), ("NLL", "nll"), ("Brier", "brier"))]
    rows += [["Model size", f"{art['tfidf']['model_bytes'] / 2**20:.1f} MB", f"{art['xlmr_original']['model_bytes'] / 2**20:.1f} MB (pretrained checkpoint; fine-tuned weights not saved)", "outputs/m1_tfidf.json, m5_xlmr.json"], ["CPU latency, historical", f"{art['tfidf']['latency_cpu_median_ms']:.1f} ms", f"{art['xlmr_original']['latency_cpu_median_ms']:.1f} ms", "same files (historical; re-measured in 4.5.7)"]]
    para("Table 10. Reconciliation of the provisional comparison (original single XLM-R run).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.6, 1.0, 1.8, 2.2], fs=7.5)
    d = ph["discrepancy"]
    para(f"The macro-F1 difference is {d['point_estimate_macro_f1_diff']:.4f} (displayed as 0.884 - 0.831 = 0.053 after rounding each score). The earlier +0.057 was the mean of the macro-F1 differences over 2,000 bootstrap resamples, which is biased because macro-F1 is non-linear: the mean over resamples is below each point estimate (XLM-R {d['bootstrap_10000']['bias_xlmr']:+.4f}, TF-IDF {d['bootstrap_10000']['bias_tfidf']:+.4f}), more so for TF-IDF, so the difference of means ({d['bootstrap_10000']['mean_diff']:.3f}) is about {d['bootstrap_10000']['mean_diff'] - d['point_estimate_macro_f1_diff']:.3f} larger. The same prediction files, the same 386 documents and the same class-inclusion rule were used in both; the point estimate is the effect size reported from here on. The discrepancy did not change the conclusion of the earlier study.", align="j")
    # ---- 4.5.2 protocol ----
    para("4.5.2 Experimental protocol and fairness", italic=True)
    ver = D.get("ver")
    for t in ("Same documents and labels. Both classifiers use the project's Task A split files (train 4,848 / validation 634 / test 629) and the same label mapping; no document identifier or template group appears in more than one split (checked), and the identical stored OCR text is the input of both (no additional normalisation).",
              f"TF-IDF baseline: re-fitted with its documented configuration and verified against the production model (maximum probability difference {D['tf']['reproduces_production']['max_abs_prob_diff_test']:.1e}, identical predictions); not retuned. Fit time {D['tf']['train_seconds']:.0f} s on one CPU thread.",
              f"XLM-R: FacebookAI/xlm-roberta-base, trainable parameters {S['per_seed'][0].get('trainable_params', 86054416):,} of 278,055,952 (the 250k-token word-embedding matrix is frozen, so this is not a full fine-tune), maximum length 256 tokens including the two special tokens, learning rate 3e-5, batch 16, bf16, at most 6 epochs, class-balanced loss. The epoch is chosen by validation macro-F1; the test set is scored once, after selection. {n} seeds ({', '.join(map(str, S['seeds']))}); each run's trainable weights (fp16), predictions, logs and configuration are stored under experiments/xlmr_vs_tfidf_final/runs/.",
              "Software: Python 3.12, PyTorch 2.9.1 (CUDA 12.8), transformers 5.18.0, scikit-learn 1.9.1; hardware: laptop NVIDIA RTX 4050 (6 GB), 16 CPU threads; exact commands and versions are saved in each run's config.json."):
        bullet(t)
    # ---- 4.5.3 seeds ----
    para("4.5.3 Multi-seed XLM-R results", italic=True)
    rows = [["Run", "Epoch", "Val macro-F1", "Test acc.", "Test macro-F1", "Delta macro-F1", "McNemar p (acc.)", "Train time", "Peak GPU (MB)"]]
    for p in S["per_seed"]: rows.append([f"seed {p['seed']}", str(p["selected_epoch"]), f3(p["val_real_macro_f1"]), f3(p["accuracy"]), f3(p["macro_f1"]), s3(p["delta_macro_f1"]), f3(p["mcnemar_exact_p"]), tsec(p["train_seconds"]), f"{p['peak_gpu_mb']:.0f}"])
    rows.append(["mean (sd)", "-", "-", f"{stb['accuracy_mean']:.3f} ({stb['accuracy_sd']:.3f})", f"{stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} ({stb['macro_f1_sd']:.3f})", s3(P["delta_macro_f1"]), "-", "-", "-"]); rows.append(["min - max", "-", "-", f"{stb['accuracy_min']:.3f} - {stb['accuracy_max']:.3f}", f"{stb['macro_f1_min']:.3f} - {stb['macro_f1_max']:.3f}", "-", "-", "-", "-"])
    rows.append(["TF-IDF + LR", "-", "-", f3(S["tfidf"]["accuracy"]), f3(S["tfidf"]["macro_f1"]), "-", "-", "38 s (CPU)", "-"]); rows.append(["XLM-R original run (not reloadable)", "6", "0.873", f3(B_["accuracy"]), f3(B_["macro_f1"]), s3(B_["macro_f1"] - A["macro_f1"]), "0.42", "405 s", "3,568"])
    para("Table 11. XLM-R (256 tokens, original recipe), one run per seed, real test documents; Delta = XLM-R minus TF-IDF. The original single run is shown for reference only; its fine-tuned model was not saved and it is not part of the statistical test.", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.6, 0.7, 0.7, 0.7, 0.6, 0.6], fs=7)
    para(f"Training stability: macro-F1 across seeds has mean {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f}, standard deviation {stb['macro_f1_sd']:.3f}, range {stb['macro_f1_min']:.3f} to {stb['macro_f1_max']:.3f}; {stb['seeds_with_positive_delta']} of {n} seeds scored above TF-IDF and {stb['seeds_with_delta_above_0_02']} of {n} by more than 0.02. "
         f"The original run (macro-F1 {B_['macro_f1']:.3f}) is {'inside' if stb['macro_f1_min'] - 0.005 <= B_['macro_f1'] <= stb['macro_f1_max'] + 0.005 else 'outside'} that range. The seeds are real repeated trainings; the bootstrap resamples below are not.", align="j")
    figure(FIG / "final_fig1_seed_macro_f1.png", 4.9, "Fig. 14. XLM-R macro-F1 for each training seed against TF-IDF (real test documents)."); figure(FIG / "final_fig4_validation_curves.png", 4.9, "Fig. 15. Validation macro-F1 per epoch for each seed; checkpoints were chosen on these curves.")
    # ---- 4.5.4 statistics ----
    para("4.5.4 Statistical comparison with TF-IDF", italic=True)
    rows = [["Quantity", "Value"], ["Absolute macro-F1 improvement (mean over seeds of per-seed differences)", s3(P["delta_macro_f1"])], ["Relative macro-F1 improvement (absolute / TF-IDF macro-F1)", f"{P['relative_macro_f1_gain_pct']:+.1f}%"],
            ["95% interval, hierarchical bootstrap (test groups AND seeds resampled, 10,000 replicates)", f"{P['ci95_hierarchical'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_hierarchical'][1]:+.3f}"], ["95% interval, test documents only (observed seeds fixed)", f"{P['ci95_documents_only'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_documents_only'][1]:+.3f}"],
            ["95% t-interval over the seed-level differences (seed variability only)", f"{P['ci95_seed_t_interval'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_seed_t_interval'][1]:+.3f}" if P["ci95_seed_t_interval"] else "N/A"], ["Absolute accuracy improvement (mean over seeds)", s3(P["delta_accuracy"])],
            ["95% hierarchical interval for the accuracy difference", f"{P['ci95_accuracy_hierarchical'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_accuracy_hierarchical'][1]:+.3f}"], ["Seed-ensemble (secondary; mean probability of all seeds)", f"accuracy {S['seed_ensemble_secondary']['accuracy']:.3f}, macro-F1 {S['seed_ensemble_secondary']['macro_f1']:.3f}"],
            ["Pre-registered decision", P["decision"]]]
    para("Table 12. Primary comparison (pre-registered): macro-F1, XLM-R (256 tokens, 5 seeds) vs TF-IDF + LR.", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[4.6, 2.2], fs=8)
    para("McNemar's exact test (per seed, Table 11) compares the number of documents only XLM-R classifies correctly against those only TF-IDF classifies correctly; it tests differences in paired correctness (accuracy), not macro-F1, and is shown only as a secondary check. "
         "The hierarchical bootstrap resamples whole template/merchant groups of the test set (the unit of dependence) and the training seeds together, so the interval reflects both finite-test-set and training-seed uncertainty; with only five seeds the seed component is itself roughly estimated.", align="j")
    para(f"Reading the intervals together: the seed-only t-interval ({P['ci95_seed_t_interval'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_seed_t_interval'][1]:+.3f}) excludes zero and every seed scored above TF-IDF, so the advantage is repeatable across training runs of this recipe; the intervals that also account for the finite test set ({P['ci95_hierarchical'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_hierarchical'][1]:+.3f} hierarchical, {P['ci95_documents_only'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_documents_only'][1]:+.3f} documents only) include zero, so the test set cannot rule out that the average advantage is a feature of these 386 documents. "
         f"Per seed, only {sum(1 for q in S['per_seed'] if q['mcnemar_exact_p'] < 0.05)} of {n} McNemar tests reached p < 0.05. This is why the pre-registered verdict is 'inconclusive'; it is not evidence that the models are equivalent.", align="j")
    figure(FIG / "final_fig2_delta_distribution.png", 4.9, "Fig. 16. Distribution of the macro-F1 difference (XLM-R - TF-IDF) over the hierarchical bootstrap; vertical lines mark zero, the 0.02 threshold and the interval.")
    # exploratory variants
    ex = [(v, D["variants"][v]) for v in ("len512", "chunk256", "ep10") if D["variants"][v]]
    if ex:
        names = {"len512": "512 tokens", "chunk256": "256-token overlapping chunks (mean logit)", "ep10": "10 epochs (256 tokens)"}; sel = (D["sel"] or {}).get("selected")
        rows = [["Exploratory variant", "Seeds", "Val macro-F1 (seed 0)", "Test macro-F1 mean", "Delta vs TF-IDF", "95% CI (documents/seeds)", "Bonferroni CI (k=3)", "Status"]]
        for v, s_ in ex:
            cands = {x["name"]: x for x in (D["sel"] or {}).get("candidates", [])}; rows.append([names[v], str(len(s_["seeds"])), f3(cands.get(v, {}).get("val")), f3(s_["stability"]["macro_f1_mean"]), s3(s_["primary"]["delta_macro_f1"]), f"{s_['primary']['ci95_hierarchical'][0]:+.3f} to {s_['primary']['ci95_hierarchical'][1]:+.3f}" if len(s_["seeds"]) > 1 else "single seed",
                         f"{s_['primary']['bonferroni_ci_hierarchical']['k3'][0]:+.3f} to {s_['primary']['bonferroni_ci_hierarchical']['k3'][1]:+.3f}" if len(s_["seeds"]) > 1 else "N/A", "selected on validation" if v == sel else "not selected"])
        para("Table 13. Exploratory input-length / training variants (not part of the primary decision; interpret with the multiple-comparison correction).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.5, 0.4, 0.7, 0.7, 0.6, 1.2, 1.1, 0.8], fs=7)
        e10 = D["variants"].get("ep10")
        if e10 and len(e10["seeds"]) > 1:
            worst = min(e10["per_seed"], key=lambda q: q["macro_f1"])
            para(f"The 10-epoch variant won the validation comparison on seed 0 (validation macro-F1 {f3(max(q['val_real_macro_f1'] for q in e10['per_seed']))} best seed) but did not generalise across seeds: mean test macro-F1 {e10['stability']['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} (sd {e10['stability']['macro_f1_sd']:.3f}) against {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} for the primary recipe, and seed {worst['seed']} stopped early at epoch {worst['selected_epoch']} with macro-F1 {worst['macro_f1']:.3f}, significantly worse than TF-IDF on accuracy (McNemar p = {worst['mcnemar_exact_p']:.3f}). Choosing the variant from one seed's validation score overstated its merit (a winner's-curse effect); it was kept out of the primary comparison, as pre-registered.", align="j")
    # ---- 4.5.5 truncation ----
    para("4.5.5 Input-truncation investigation", italic=True)
    t = tr; sh = t["share_truncated"]; dd = t["discarded_test_real_256"]; ti = t.get("tail_information_production_tfidf", {}); dg = t["tfidf_same_truncation_diagnostic"]
    for x in (f"Mechanics: the 256-token limit includes the two special tokens (<s>, </s>), so 254 content tokens remain; truncation keeps the START of the text ({t['tokenizer']['truncation_side']}-side cut) and discards the end. At 512 tokens 510 content tokens remain.",
              f"Prevalence (token count including special tokens): {sh['test']['over_256']['real_pct']:.1f}% of real test documents exceed 256 tokens ({sh['test']['over_256']['all_pct']:.1f}% of all test documents) and {sh['test']['over_512']['real_pct']:.1f}% exceed 512; for training documents {sh['train']['over_256']['real_pct']:.1f}% (real) exceed 256. The earlier figures (47.4% and 33.7% for the BiLSTM's word limit) were reproduced.",
              f"What is lost: among the {dd['n_docs']} truncated real test documents the cut removes on average {100 * dd['mean_frac_tokens_lost']:.0f}% of the tokens; the discarded text mentions total/tax words in {dd['tail_mentions_total_tax_words_pct']:.0f}% of them (the retained part in {dd['head_mentions_total_tax_words_pct']:.0f}%).",
              f"Does the tail carry class information? The production TF-IDF model scores {100 * ti['accuracy_full_text']:.1f}% on those documents with the full text and {100 * ti['accuracy_head_only_256']:.1f}% with only the first 256 tokens, and {100 * ti['accuracy_tail_only']:.1f}% from the discarded tail alone: the tail is informative but largely redundant with the head for TF-IDF.",
              f"Same-policy diagnostic (TF-IDF re-fitted on text cut at the same token limit): at 256 tokens macro-F1 {dg['tfidf_256']['real_macro_f1']:.3f} / accuracy {dg['tfidf_256']['real_accuracy']:.3f}; at 512 tokens {dg['tfidf_512']['real_macro_f1']:.3f} / {dg['tfidf_512']['real_accuracy']:.3f}; full text {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f} / {S['tfidf']['accuracy']:.3f}. TF-IDF therefore loses {S['tfidf']['macro_f1'] - dg['tfidf_256']['real_macro_f1']:.3f} macro-F1 when it too is limited to 256 tokens: truncation costs something, but little compared with the difference between the models."):
        bullet(x)
    var_txt = []
    for v, s_ in ex:
        cands = {x["name"]: x for x in (D["sel"] or {}).get("candidates", [])}; var_txt.append(f"{names[v]}: validation macro-F1 {f3(cands.get(v, {}).get('val'))}, test macro-F1 {f3(s_['stability']['macro_f1_mean'])} ({len(s_['seeds'])} seed{'s' if len(s_['seeds']) > 1 else ''})")
    s0 = S["per_seed"][0]; l512 = D["variants"].get("len512")
    if var_txt: para("Does longer or chunked input help XLM-R? " + "; ".join(var_txt) + f". For comparison the 256-token recipe with the same seed (0) scored validation {f3(s0['val_real_macro_f1'])} and test macro-F1 {f3(s0['macro_f1'])} (five-seed mean {f3(stb['macro_f1_mean'])}). " + (f"The 512-token run's test macro-F1 ({f3(l512['stability']['macro_f1_mean'])}) is not higher than the same-seed 256-token run, and it needed {tsec(c['training']['len512']['seconds_mean'])} to train against {tsec(s0['train_seconds'])}. " if l512 else "") + "Truncation is therefore not shown to be the cause of any gap: longer or chunked input did not raise the test score in these single-seed exploratory runs, and TF-IDF itself loses only about 0.017 macro-F1 at 256 tokens.", align="j")
    figure(FIG / "final_fig5_truncation_by_class.png", 5.4, "Fig. 17. Share of real test documents longer than the 256-token (and 512-token) limit, by class; electricity, internet and miscellaneous are almost always truncated, so the neural model sees only their first part.")
    # ---- 4.5.6 per class ----
    para("4.5.6 Per-class performance", italic=True)
    rows = [["Category", "Test n", "TF-IDF F1", "XLM-R F1 mean (min-max)", "Delta mean", "Seeds above TF-IDF"]]
    for k, v in S["per_class"].items(): rows.append([k, str(v["support"]), f3(v["tfidf_f1"]), f"{v['xlmr_f1_mean']:.3f} ({v['xlmr_f1_min']:.2f}-{v['xlmr_f1_max']:.2f})", s3(v["delta_mean"]), f"{v['seeds_better']}/{n}"])
    para("Table 14. Per-class F1 on the real test documents (macro-F1 averages these 15 values; classes with n < 15 are noisy).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.3, 0.5, 0.8, 1.6, 0.8, 1.0], fs=7.5)
    gains = sorted(S["per_class"].items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]["delta_mean"]); small = [k for k, v in S["per_class"].items() if v["support"] < 15]; gs = sum(v["delta_mean"] for k, v in S["per_class"].items() if k in small) / len(S["per_class"])
    top = gains[0]; top_c = top[1]["delta_mean"] / len(S["per_class"])
    para(f"Largest mean gains: {', '.join(f'{k} ({s3(v['delta_mean'])}, n={v['support']})' for k, v in gains[:3])}; largest mean losses: {', '.join(f'{k} ({s3(v['delta_mean'])}, n={v['support']})' for k, v in gains[-3:])}. Classes with fewer than 15 test documents account for {gs:+.3f} of the {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f} macro-F1 difference ({100 * gs / P['delta_macro_f1']:.0f}% of it). The single class '{top[0]}' ({top[1]['support']} test documents) contributes {top_c:+.3f} on its own ({100 * top_c / P['delta_macro_f1']:.0f}% of the total): the macro-F1 advantage is driven substantially by classes with very few test documents, whereas on the large classes (food, non_expense, miscellaneous) the differences are small and not consistently in XLM-R's favour.", align="j")
    figure(FIG / "final_fig3_per_class.png", 5.8, "Fig. 18. Per-class F1: TF-IDF versus XLM-R (mean of seeds, whiskers min-max).")
    # ---- 4.5.7 resources ----
    para("4.5.7 Resource and latency comparison", italic=True)
    order = [k for k in ("tfidf", "xlmr", "bilstm", "bilstm_attn", "layoutlmv3") if k in m]; tt = c["training"]
    def trn(k):
        key = {"xlmr": "primary256"}.get(k, k); v = tt.get(key); return tsec(v["seconds_mean"]) + (" (hist.)" if v and "HISTORICAL" in v.get("source", "") else "") if v else "N/A"
    rows = [["Model", "Artifact (MB)", "CPU e2e (ms)", "CPU model-only (ms)", "GPU e2e (ms)", "Throughput CPU bs32 (doc/s)", "Peak RSS (MB)", "Peak GPU bs32 (MB)", "Training"]]
    for k in order:
        x = m[k]; rows.append([x["name"].replace(" (fine-tuned, 256 tokens)", ""), mb(x["artifact_mb"]), ms(x["cpu_end_to_end_ms"]), ms(x["cpu_model_only_ms"]), ms(x["gpu_end_to_end_ms"]), f"{x['throughput_cpu']['32']:.1f}" if x["throughput_cpu"] else "N/A", f"{x['rss_peak_mb']:.0f}", f"{x['gpu_peak_mb_b32']:.0f}" if x["gpu_peak_mb_b32"] else "N/A", trn(k)])
    para("Table 15. Measured resource use (same machine, 4 CPU threads, first 100 real test documents; end-to-end = tokenisation/vectorisation + forward + softmax, OCR excluded; GPU Transformers in bf16; training times for BiLSTM/LayoutLMv3 are historical).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.3, 0.55, 0.55, 0.6, 0.55, 0.7, 0.55, 0.6, 0.8], fs=6.5)
    para("Deployment dependencies: TF-IDF needs scikit-learn/numpy only and a 48 MB file; XLM-R needs PyTorch, transformers, tokenizers/sentencepiece and safetensors, the 1.06 GB pretrained base weights plus the 172 MB fine-tuned delta, and a model-loading step (see the load times and RSS in the saved benchmark JSON); LayoutLMv3 additionally needs the page image and OCR word boxes at inference. These are measured file sizes and package versions, not estimates.", align="j")
    figure(FIG / "final_fig6_performance_vs_cost.png", 5.0, "Fig. 19. Macro-F1 against CPU end-to-end latency (log scale); bubble area grows with model size.")
    return D

def worth_it(para, bullet, table, figure, D):
    S, C = D["S"], claims(D["S"]); P = S["primary"]; stb = S["stability"]; c, r, m = cost_facts(D); tf, xl = m["tfidf"], m["xlmr"]; sc = c["scenarios_seconds"]
    para("4.5.8 Classification Performance Versus Computational Cost: Is XLM-RoBERTa Worth It?", italic=True)
    para("A. How much does XLM-R gain? ", bold=True, size=11)
    para(f"Absolute macro-F1 {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f} (relative {P['relative_macro_f1_gain_pct']:+.1f}%), interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}; accuracy {P['delta_accuracy']:+.3f} (interval {P['ci95_accuracy_hierarchical'][0]:+.3f} to {P['ci95_accuracy_hierarchical'][1]:+.3f}). "
         f"{stb['seeds_with_positive_delta']} of {len(S['seeds'])} seeds beat TF-IDF; seed-to-seed macro-F1 sd {stb['macro_f1_sd']:.3f}. The gain is concentrated in classes with few test documents (Table 14). Statistical status: {P['decision']}.", align="j")
    para("B. What does it cost? ", bold=True, size=11)
    rows = [["Cost measure", "TF-IDF", "XLM-R", "Ratio (XLM-R / TF-IDF)"], ["Model artifact", f"{mb(tf['artifact_mb'])} MB", f"{mb(xl['artifact_mb'])} MB", f"{r['artifact_size']:.1f}x"], ["CPU latency, model-only (ms/doc)", ms(tf["cpu_model_only_ms"]), ms(xl["cpu_model_only_ms"]), f"{xl['cpu_model_only_ms'] / tf['cpu_model_only_ms']:.0f}x"],
            ["CPU latency, end-to-end (ms/doc)", ms(tf["cpu_end_to_end_ms"]), ms(xl["cpu_end_to_end_ms"]), f"{r['cpu_end_to_end']:.0f}x"], ["GPU latency, end-to-end (ms/doc)", "not applicable (CPU model)", ms(xl["gpu_end_to_end_ms"]), f"{xl['gpu_end_to_end_ms'] / tf['cpu_end_to_end_ms']:.1f}x vs TF-IDF on CPU"],
            ["Throughput at batch 32, CPU (docs/s)", f"{tf['throughput_cpu']['32']:.1f}", f"{xl['throughput_cpu']['32']:.1f}", f"{tf['throughput_cpu']['32'] / xl['throughput_cpu']['32']:.0f}x slower"], ["Throughput at batch 32, GPU (docs/s)", "-", f"{xl['throughput_gpu']['32']:.1f}", "-"],
            ["Peak CPU RSS (MB)", f"{tf['rss_peak_mb']:.0f}", f"{xl['rss_peak_mb']:.0f}", f"{r['rss_peak']:.1f}x"], ["Peak GPU memory, inference batch 32 (MB)", "none", f"{xl['gpu_peak_mb_b32']:.0f}", "-"], ["Training time", tsec(c["training"]["tfidf"]["seconds_mean"]), tsec(c["training"]["primary256"]["seconds_mean"]) + " per seed (GPU)", f"{c['training']['primary256']['seconds_mean'] / c['training']['tfidf']['seconds_mean']:.0f}x"]]
    para(f"Table 16. Measured cost of XLM-R relative to TF-IDF. XLM-R artifact = {sum(v for k_, v in xl['artifact_files'].items() if 'xlm-roberta' in k_) / 2**20:.0f} MB pretrained base weights + {sum(v for k_, v in xl['artifact_files'].items() if 'trainable' in k_) / 2**20:.0f} MB fine-tuned delta as stored; one merged fp32 file would be about {c['xlmr_merged_fp32_checkpoint_mb']:.0f} MB ({c['xlmr_merged_vs_tfidf_ratio']:.0f}x).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[2.4, 1.4, 1.7, 1.5], fs=8)
    para("C. Is the gain worth the cost? ", bold=True, size=11)
    rows = [["Documents", "TF-IDF, CPU (batch 1)", "XLM-R, CPU (batch 1)", "XLM-R, GPU (batch 1)", "XLM-R, GPU (batch 32)"]]
    for k, v in sc.items(): rows.append([f"{int(k):,}", tsec(v["tfidf"]["cpu_end_to_end_batch1_s"]), tsec(v["xlmr"]["cpu_end_to_end_batch1_s"]), tsec(v["xlmr"]["gpu_end_to_end_batch1_s"]), tsec(v["xlmr"]["gpu_throughput_bs32_s"])])
    para("Table 17. ESTIMATED classification time (number of documents x measured median latency / throughput; OCR, model loading and queueing excluded).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.0, 1.5, 1.5, 1.5, 1.5], fs=8)
    figure(FIG / "final_fig7_scenarios.png", 4.9, "Fig. 20. Estimated time to classify 1,000 / 10,000 / 100,000 documents (log scale).")
    s100 = sc["100000"]; para(f"For 100,000 documents the estimate is {tsec(s100['tfidf']['cpu_end_to_end_batch1_s'])} for TF-IDF on a CPU against {tsec(s100['xlmr']['cpu_end_to_end_batch1_s'])} for XLM-R on a CPU (batch 1) or {tsec(s100['xlmr']['gpu_throughput_bs32_s'])} on this GPU at batch 32. "
         f"Whether these times are acceptable depends on an application requirement that was not specified for this project and is therefore not assumed. The measurements show that XLM-R needs the GPU to be practical at volume, and that even then it is {r['gpu_end_to_end_vs_tfidf_cpu']:.1f} times slower per document (batch 1) and delivers {xl['throughput_gpu']['32']:.0f} documents/s at batch 32 against {tf['throughput_cpu']['32']:.0f} for TF-IDF on the CPU.", align="j")
    # decision matrix
    qual = f"mean macro-F1 {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} vs {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f}"; cal_t, cal_x = S["tfidf"], stb
    pcal = [p["ece_cal"] for p in S["per_seed"]]; nll_x = sum(p["nll_cal"] for p in S["per_seed"]) / len(S["per_seed"]); bri = sum(p["brier_cal"] for p in S["per_seed"]) / len(S["per_seed"])
    rows = [["Criterion", "TF-IDF", "XLM-RoBERTa", "Evidence-based assessment"],
            ["Classification quality", f"macro-F1 {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f}, acc {S['tfidf']['accuracy']:.3f}", f"macro-F1 {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} (sd {stb['macro_f1_sd']:.3f}), acc {stb['accuracy_mean']:.3f}", "XLM-R higher on average" if C["observed"] else "TF-IDF higher or equal"],
            ["Statistical certainty", "-", f"interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}", "superiority supported" + (", above 0.02" if C["prac"] else ", not above 0.02") if C["stat"] else "inconclusive: interval includes zero"],
            ["CPU latency (end-to-end)", f"{ms(tf['cpu_end_to_end_ms'])} ms", f"{ms(xl['cpu_end_to_end_ms'])} ms", f"XLM-R {r['cpu_end_to_end']:.0f}x slower"], ["GPU requirements", "none", f"training {c['training']['primary256']['gpu_peak_mb_mean']:.0f} MB; inference batch 32 {xl['gpu_peak_mb_b32']:.0f} MB", "GPU needed for training; strongly advisable for inference"],
            ["Model size", f"{mb(tf['artifact_mb'])} MB", f"{mb(xl['artifact_mb'])} MB", f"{r['artifact_size']:.0f}x larger"], ["Peak memory (CPU RSS)", f"{tf['rss_peak_mb']:.0f} MB", f"{xl['rss_peak_mb']:.0f} MB", f"{r['rss_peak']:.1f}x"],
            ["Training cost", tsec(c["training"]["tfidf"]["seconds_mean"]) + " (CPU)", tsec(c["training"]["primary256"]["seconds_mean"]) + " per run (GPU)", f"{c['training']['primary256']['seconds_mean'] / c['training']['tfidf']['seconds_mean']:.0f}x; XLM-R needs several seeds"],
            ["Calibration (temperature-scaled)", f"ECE {S['tfidf']['ece']:.3f}, NLL {S['tfidf']['nll']:.3f}", f"ECE {sum(pcal) / len(pcal):.3f}, NLL {nll_x:.3f}", "TF-IDF better calibrated" if S["tfidf"]["ece"] < sum(pcal) / len(pcal) else "XLM-R better calibrated"],
            ["Deployment complexity", "one small file, scikit-learn", "PyTorch + transformers + 1.2 GB of weights, tokeniser, GPU for speed", "XLM-R clearly higher"],
            ["Overall feasibility", "feasible on any CPU", "feasible on CPU for low volumes, GPU for high volumes", ("XLM-R: promising but not yet proven superior" if not C["stat"] else ("XLM-R: superior in macro-F1 and practically meaningful; cost is large - worth it only where volume/hardware allow" if C["prac"] else "XLM-R: statistically superior but the gain is below the practical threshold, not worth the cost under the measured conditions"))]]
    para("Table 18. Decision matrix.", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.3, 1.5, 2.0, 2.2], fs=7.5)
    para("D. Four different statements. ", bold=True, size=11)
    for t in (f"1. XLM-R achieved a higher observed score: {'yes' if C['observed'] else 'no'} (macro-F1 {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} vs {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f}; accuracy {stb['accuracy_mean']:.3f} vs {S['tfidf']['accuracy']:.3f}).", f"2. XLM-R demonstrated statistically supported superiority: {'yes' if C['stat'] else 'no'} (interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}).",
              f"3. XLM-R demonstrated a practically meaningful improvement (lower bound above 0.02): {'yes' if C['prac'] else 'no'}.", f"4. XLM-R provides a better overall production trade-off: {'not demonstrated' if not C['stat'] else 'not demonstrated either - the measured costs are large and depend on deployment constraints that were not specified'}."):
        bullet(t)
    return D


def others_and_end(para, bullet, table, figure, D):
    S, C, om, pw = D["S"], claims(D["S"]), D["om"], D["pw"]; P = S["primary"]; stb = S["stability"]; c, r, m = cost_facts(D); n = len(S["seeds"])
    # ---- 4.5.9 other models ----
    para("4.5.9 Why the other models scored lower", italic=True)
    para("For each model the observed shortfall, the facts that were measured, the explanations that remain unverified and the experiment that would be needed to establish a cause are kept apart. None of this shows that RNNs, attention or layout-aware Transformers are inherently inferior: these were small-data baselines trained once under limited budgets.", align="j")
    for key in ("bilstm", "bilstm_attn", "layoutlmv3", "lexrank"):
        o = om[key]; ms_ = o["measured"]; ob = o["observed"]
        if key.startswith("bilstm"):
            facts = (f"{ms_['params']:,} parameters, embeddings learned from scratch on {ms_['train_docs']:,} documents; epochs run per seed {ms_['epochs_run_per_seed']} (best epochs {ms_['best_epoch_per_seed']}, maximum {ms_['max_epochs']}); test accuracy across seeds {', '.join(f'{x:.3f}' for x in ms_['seed_test_accuracy'])}; {100 * ms_['oov_share_of_alphabetic_tokens']:.0f}% of alphabetic test tokens are outside the vocabulary; "
                     f"{ms_['truncation_over_256_word_tokens_real_test_pct']:.0f}% of real test documents exceed the 256-word limit.")
        elif key == "layoutlmv3": facts = (f"{ms_['epochs']} epochs at maximum length {ms_['train_max_len']} (inference {ms_['inference_max_len']}); validation macro-F1 by epoch {ms_['val_real_macro_f1_by_epoch']} (still rising at the last epoch); {ms_['real_test_docs_over_448_tokens_pct']:.0f}% of real test documents exceed 448 tokens; accuracy is within {abs(ob['delta_accuracy_vs_tfidf']):.3f} of TF-IDF while macro-F1 is {abs(ob['delta_macro_f1_vs_tfidf']):.3f} lower.")
        else: facts = "; ".join((f"{int(100 * s['frac'])}% of lines" if s["frac"] else "full flat OCR text") + f": macro-F1 {s['macro_f1']:.3f} ({s['reduction_pct']:.0f}% less text)" for s in ms_["sweep"]) + "; random 50% of lines " + str(next((f"{c_['macro_f1']:.3f}" for c_ in ms_["controls"] if "random 50" in c_["config"]), "N/A")) + ", first 50% " + str(next((f"{c_['macro_f1']:.3f}" for c_ in ms_["controls"] if "first lines 50" in c_["config"]), "N/A")) + "."
        para(f"{o['name']}: (1) Observed: macro-F1 {ob.get('macro_f1', ob.get('macro_f1_50pct')):.3f}, {s3(ob.get('delta_macro_f1_vs_tfidf', ob.get('delta_macro_f1_vs_full_text')))} relative to TF-IDF. (2) Measured: {facts} (3) Unverified explanations: " + " ".join(f"({chr(97 + i)}) {h}" for i, h in enumerate(o["unverified_hypotheses"])).replace("(see classes_with_largest_loss_vs_tfidf)", "(largest losses: " + ", ".join(f"{q['cls']} {q['delta']:+.2f} (n={q['support']})" for q in o["measured"].get("classes_with_largest_loss_vs_tfidf", [])[:3]) + ")") + " (4) Experiments needed: " + " ".join(f"({chr(97 + i)}) {h}" for i, h in enumerate(o["experiments_needed"])), align="j")
    # ---- 4.5.10 sample size ----
    para("4.5.10 Is the test set large enough?", italic=True)
    ac = pw["accuracy_mcnemar"]; sim = pw["macro_f1_simulation"]["rows"]; pc = pw["per_class"]
    para(f"The real test set has 386 documents (382 template groups); {len(pc['classes_below_15'])} classes have fewer than 15 documents ({', '.join(f'{a} {b}' for a, b in pc['classes_below_15'])}), so macro-F1, which weights every class equally, is dominated by noisy small classes. "
         f"Estimate 1 (analytic, accuracy): with the observed discordant-pair rate of {100 * ac['observed_discordant_rate']:.1f}% (documents classified differently by the two models), a McNemar test with alpha 0.05 and 80% power needs about {ac['n_documents_needed']['accuracy_gain_0.01']:,} documents to detect a true accuracy gain of 0.01, {ac['n_documents_needed']['accuracy_gain_0.02']:,} for 0.02 and {ac['n_documents_needed']['accuracy_gain_0.03']:,} for 0.03 (independent documents assumed). "
         f"Estimate 2 (macro-F1, resampling simulation assuming the observed seed-mean effect of {sim and pw['macro_f1_simulation']['observed_delta']:+.3f} is the true effect, which is optimistic): the probability that the interval's lower bound exceeds zero is {', '.join(f'{100 * x['power_lower_ci_above_0']:.0f}% at {x['n_documents']:,} documents' for x in sim)}. "
         f"At the current class mix, a test set with at least 30 documents in the rarest class would need about {pc['documents_needed_for_30_per_class_at_current_mix']:,} documents; collecting the rare classes on purpose needs far fewer. These are planning estimates with stated assumptions, not measured results; five or ten seeds do not remove the need for a larger, frozen, template-disjoint test set kept out of training and model selection.", align="j")
    # ---- 4.5.11 reproducibility ----
    para("4.5.11 Reproducibility and limitations", italic=True)
    import numpy as np
    RB = J(O / "resources/xlmr_primary256_s0.json")["checkpoint_reproduces_stored_predictions"]
    orig = np.load(ROOT / "experiments/text_vs_neural/outputs/xlmr_logits.npz")["test"]; new0 = np.load(FIN / "runs/primary256_s0/logits.npz")["test"]
    agree = float((orig.argmax(1) == new0.argmax(1)).mean())
    for t in (f"Original run: its fine-tuned weights were never saved, so it cannot be reloaded or re-evaluated; only its stored logits exist. A fresh run with the same seed and recipe agrees with it on {100 * agree:.1f}% of test-document predictions but gives macro-F1 {S['per_seed'][0]['macro_f1']:.3f} against 0.884 (it selected epoch {S['per_seed'][0]['selected_epoch']}, the original epoch 6): across the five seeds the macro-F1 ranges from {stb['macro_f1_min']:.3f} to {stb['macro_f1_max']:.3f} (a spread of {stb['macro_f1_max'] - stb['macro_f1_min']:.3f}, comparable to the {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f} advantage), which is why all conclusions here rest on the multi-seed analysis rather than on any single run.",
              f"New runs: for each of the {n} seeds the trainable weights (fp16, 172 MB), tokenizer reference, configuration, per-epoch history, validation/test logits and the log are stored; the pretrained base weights (1.06 GB) are shared and checksum-verified. The stored predictions come from the fp32/bf16 in-memory model at training time; reloading the fp16 trainable weights in a fresh process (CPU, fp32) reproduces the stored predictions for {int(round(100 * RB["same_argmax_share"]))} of 100 test documents with a maximum logit difference of {RB["max_abs_logit_diff"]:.2f}. GPU bf16 training is not bit-reproducible, so re-training will give similar but not identical numbers.",
              "Number of seeds: five were pre-registered and form the primary analysis. Seeds 5-9 were added later, after the five-seed result was known and disk space had been freed, as a secondary sensitivity analysis (Section 4.5.13); they cannot change the primary verdict. Exploratory variants (512 tokens, chunking) are single seeds.",
              "Not measured: peak CPU RSS during training; GPU power; the effect of quantisation or ONNX export on latency; behaviour on documents from new templates (the test set has none by construction of the group split).",
              "Latency/memory: all models were measured on one shared laptop (one CPU, one 6 GB GPU) in separate processes with 4 CPU threads, while the demo servers were idle-resident; absolute values will differ elsewhere, the ratios are the evidence."):
        bullet(t)
    # ---- 4.5.12 recommendation ----
    para("4.5.12 Final evidence-based recommendation", italic=True)
    cr = r["cpu_end_to_end"]
    if C["stat"] and C["prac"]:
        rec = f"XLM-R is a better classifier here by a statistically supported and practically meaningful margin (macro-F1 {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f}, interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}), but it is {cr:.0f} times slower per document on the CPU and {r['artifact_size']:.0f} times larger. Use it where a GPU (or a low document volume) is available; keep TF-IDF where CPU-only, low-latency or low-footprint operation matters. A hybrid (TF-IDF first, XLM-R for low-confidence documents) was not tested."
    elif C["stat"]:
        rec = f"XLM-R's macro-F1 advantage ({P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f}, interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}) is statistically supported but not shown to exceed the 0.02 practical threshold; with {cr:.0f} times the CPU latency and {r['artifact_size']:.0f} times the size, the cost is not justified by the measured gain. Keep TF-IDF + logistic regression in production."
    elif C["inferior"]: rec = "XLM-R is worse than TF-IDF under the evaluated conditions; keep TF-IDF."
    else:
        rec = (f"The evidence is inconclusive: XLM-R scored higher on average (macro-F1 {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f} over {n} seeds) but the 95% interval ({C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}) includes zero, so superiority is not established, and it costs {cr:.0f} times the CPU latency and {r['artifact_size']:.0f} times the model size. "
               "Keep TF-IDF + logistic regression as the production text classifier. XLM-R remains a promising candidate; the decisive next step is a larger, frozen, template-disjoint test set (Section 4.5.10) rather than more tuning on the current one. Inconclusive does not mean equivalent.")
    para(rec, align="j")
    unseen_section(para, bullet, table, figure, D)
    hybrid_section(para, bullet, table, figure, D)


def abstract_line(D):
    S, C = D["S"], claims(D["S"]); P = S["primary"]; stb = S["stability"]; c, r, m = cost_facts(D)
    s = (f"A final multi-seed study of the transformer XLM-RoBERTa (XLM-R) against TF-IDF found a mean macro-F1 of {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} versus {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f} (difference {P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f}, 95% interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}), i.e. "
         + ("a statistically supported" + (" and practically meaningful" if C["prac"] else " but not practically meaningful") + " advantage" if C["stat"] else "an advantage that the test set cannot establish (inconclusive, not equivalence)")
         + f", at {r['cpu_end_to_end']:.0f} times the CPU latency and {r['artifact_size']:.0f} times the model size.")
    if D.get("ten") and D.get("usyn") and D.get("ureal"): s += " A ten-seed analysis, two unseen test sets and a TF-IDF-first hybrid did not change this verdict."
    return s

def conclusion_line(D):
    S, C = D["S"], claims(D["S"]); P = S["primary"]; stb = S["stability"]; c, r, m = cost_facts(D)
    return (f"Final XLM-R study (Section 4.5, {len(S['seeds'])} seeds, pre-registered rule): {P['decision']}. XLM-R reached macro-F1 {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} on average against {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f} for TF-IDF ({P['delta_macro_f1']:+.3f}; interval {C['lo']:+.3f} to {C['hi']:+.3f}); all {stb['seeds_with_positive_delta']} of {len(S['seeds'])} seeds scored higher, but the finite real test set (386 documents) does not establish superiority"
            if not C["stat"] else f"Final XLM-R study (Section 4.5): {P['decision']}; mean macro-F1 {stb['macro_f1_mean']:.3f} vs {S['tfidf']['macro_f1']:.3f}") + f"; its cost is {r['cpu_end_to_end']:.0f} times the CPU latency and {r['artifact_size']:.0f} times the size, so TF-IDF + logistic regression remains the recommended production classifier until a larger test set decides the question."


def unseen_section(para, bullet, table, figure, D):
    if not (D.get("ten") and D.get("usyn") and D.get("ureal")): return
    f3_ = lambda x: f"{x:.3f}"; sg = lambda x: f"{x:+.3f}"; ci = lambda a: f"{a[0]:+.3f} to {a[1]:+.3f}"
    S5, S10 = D["S"], D["ten"]; P5, P10 = S5["primary"], S10["primary"]
    para("4.5.13 Ten-seed sensitivity analysis", italic=True)
    rows = [["", "Seeds", "XLM-R mean macro-F1", "Difference vs TF-IDF", "95% hierarchical interval", "95% interval, seeds only", "Seeds above TF-IDF", "Verdict"]]
    for nm, S_, P_ in (("Primary (pre-registered)", S5, P5), ("Sensitivity (seeds 0-9)", S10, P10)):
        rows.append([nm, str(len(S_["seeds"])), f3_(S_["stability"]["macro_f1_mean"]), sg(P_["delta_macro_f1"]), ci(P_["ci95_hierarchical"]), ci(P_["ci95_seed_t_interval"]), f"{S_['stability']['seeds_with_positive_delta']} of {len(S_['seeds'])}", P_["decision"].split(":")[0]])
    para("Table 19. Primary five-seed analysis and the ten-seed sensitivity analysis (real test documents, n = 386).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.4, 0.4, 0.8, 0.8, 1.0, 1.0, 0.7, 0.5], fs=7)
    para(f"Five more seeds (5-9) of the same recipe were trained after the five-seed result was known, because disk space had been freed. Because adding seeds after an inconclusive result is a form of optional stopping, this analysis is labelled secondary and was declared in the pre-registration addendum before any extra seed was trained; the primary verdict remains the five-seed one. Result (verified): the mean difference is {sg(P10['delta_macro_f1'])}, the hierarchical interval {ci(P10['ci95_hierarchical'])} still includes zero, and all {S10['stability']['seeds_with_positive_delta']} of {len(S10['seeds'])} seeds score above TF-IDF. The interval that reflects only seed variation excludes zero ({ci(P10['ci95_seed_t_interval'])}), but it ignores the finite test set. More seeds therefore reduce training-run uncertainty and leave test-set uncertainty (documents and templates) unchanged: the verdict is not expected to change with further seeds.", align="j")
    para("4.5.14 Unseen test sets", italic=True)
    U, R, ux, rx = D["usyn"], D["ureal"], D["uxs"], D["uxr"]; A = U["analyses"]; Rr = R["analyses"]["primary_classes_ge10_seeds0to4"]; R10 = R["analyses"]["primary_classes_ge10_seeds0to9"]
    para("Because the real test set is small, additional unseen documents were sought. Every labelled real source already on disk is part of the project data, and a web search found no open dataset with expense-category labels for the 16 classes (utilities, rent, fuel and parking in particular). Two sets were therefore evaluated, both scored once with frozen models (TF-IDF refit, XLM-R seeds; no tuning, calibration or selection) under an analysis plan written before scoring (pre-registration addenda 2 and 3):", align="j")
    bullet(f"Synthetic challenge set (unseen_synthetic/, {U['meta']['n']} documents, 16 classes, {A['pooled_seeds0to4']['n_groups']} groups): produced by a new generator independent of the project's synthetic training generator (new merchants, item vocabulary, titles, field labels, four layouts), in three conditions - clean pages, noisy photographs (rotation, blur, JPEG compression) and long pages with boilerplate before the informative lines - and read with the same RapidOCR engine. As pre-registered, no document was removed: {U['meta']['ocr_empty']} noisy photographs produced no OCR text at all and are kept (both models see the same empty text, so they are scored on it as on any other document). Labels are correct by construction, but the documents were written by the study author: the set measures robustness to wording, layout, noise and length shift, not real-world accuracy.")
    bullet(f"Real Portuguese set (unseen_real/, {R['meta']['n']} documents): images of Zenodo record 6371710 (Cruz and Castelli, CC BY 4.0) that the project does not use, excluding every company that also appears in the project's subset. Labels are rule-derived from the dataset's own company field (merchant type), i.e. silver labels. Only two classes have at least 10 documents (miscellaneous {Rr['per_class']['miscellaneous']['support']}, food {Rr['per_class']['food']['support']}), so a 16-class macro-F1 is impossible; the primary measure is macro-F1 over those two classes, with errors into any of the 16 labels counted. The documents are in Portuguese, a language the models were not trained on, so this is a domain-shift test and mixes several effects.")
    rows = [["Set / condition", "Documents", "Groups", "TF-IDF", "TF-IDF, first 256 tokens", "XLM-R (5 seeds)", "Difference", "95% hierarchical interval", "Verdict"]]
    for nm, key in (("Synthetic, clean", "clean"), ("Synthetic, noisy photo", "noisy"), ("Synthetic, long preamble", "long"), ("Synthetic, all", "pooled")):
        a = A[f"{key}_seeds0to4"]; rows.append([nm, str(a["n_docs"]), str(a["n_groups"]), f3_(a["tfidf"]["macro_f1"]), f3_(a["tfidf_head256"]["macro_f1"]), f3_(a["xlmr"]["macro_f1_mean"]), sg(a["delta_macro_f1"]), ci(a["ci95_hierarchical"]), a["decision"].split(":")[0]])
    rows.append(["Real Portuguese (2 classes)", str(Rr["n_docs"]), str(Rr["n_groups"]), f3_(Rr["tfidf"]["macro_f1"]), f3_(Rr["tfidf_head256"]["macro_f1"]), f3_(Rr["xlmr"]["macro_f1_mean"]), sg(Rr["delta_macro_f1"]), ci(Rr["ci95_hierarchical"]), Rr["decision"].split(":")[0]])
    para("Table 20. Frozen models on the unseen sets (macro-F1; the five pre-registered seeds; A+B = practically meaningful advantage, C = inconclusive under the pre-registered rule; these verdicts apply to these sets only).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.3, 0.55, 0.45, 0.5, 0.65, 0.6, 0.55, 1.0, 0.45], fs=7)
    figure(FIG / "final_fig8_unseen_sets.png", 5.6, "Fig. 21. Macro-F1 on the main real test set and on the unseen sets; labels give the XLM-R minus TF-IDF difference and its 95% hierarchical interval.")
    lg = ux["long"]; cl = ux["clean"]; ten_s = U["analyses"]["pooled_seeds0to9"]
    para(f"What is verified. (1) On clean and noisy synthetic documents XLM-R is ahead of TF-IDF with intervals that exclude zero ({sg(A['clean_seeds0to4']['delta_macro_f1'])} and {sg(A['noisy_seeds0to4']['delta_macro_f1'])}); the ten-seed pooled difference is {sg(ten_s['delta_macro_f1'])}. (2) The long-preamble condition does not separate the models: {100 * lg['non_expense_pred_share_tfidf']:.0f}% of its documents are predicted non_expense by TF-IDF and {100 * lg['non_expense_pred_share_xlmr_mean']:.0f}% (mean over seeds) by XLM-R, although only {100 * lg['true_non_expense_share']:.0f}% are non_expense, so both fail because the generic boilerplate resembles a letter or notice. This condition therefore does not isolate truncation (applying the same 256-token cut to TF-IDF changes its macro-F1 from {f3_(A['long_seeds0to4']['tfidf']['macro_f1'])} to {f3_(A['long_seeds0to4']['tfidf_head256']['macro_f1'])}), and the small XLM-R lead there ({sg(A['long_seeds0to4']['delta_macro_f1'])}) sits on a floor. (3) On the real Portuguese documents both models are poor (macro-F1 {f3_(Rr['tfidf']['macro_f1'])} for TF-IDF, {f3_(Rr['xlmr']['macro_f1_mean'])} for XLM-R), the difference is inconclusive ({ci(Rr['ci95_hierarchical'])}), and XLM-R accuracy varies strongly between seeds ({min(R10['xlmr']['accuracy_per_seed']):.2f} to {max(R10['xlmr']['accuracy_per_seed']):.2f} over ten seeds), so single-seed results on out-of-domain documents are unreliable.", align="j")
    para(f"What is not verified (hypotheses). The clean-condition gain may arise because TF-IDF depends on training-set wording - on the clean set TF-IDF predicts food for {100 * cl['food_pred_share_tfidf']:.0f}% of documents against {100 * cl['food_pred_share_xlmr_mean']:.0f}% for XLM-R although every class has the same number of documents - while XLM-R uses the meaning of words learned in pre-training; no ablation was run to test this. The noisy-condition gain could instead reflect different sensitivity to OCR errors. Neither hypothesis was tested, and the real Portuguese set could not confirm or reject them. Label caveat: the project's own labels treat groceries as miscellaneous, but its models may have learned to call groceries food; the Portuguese labels follow the project's convention, so part of the poor real-set scores may be a labelling-convention mismatch rather than a model failure.", align="j")
    para("Consequence for the conclusion. The pre-registered rule applied to the synthetic set gives a practically meaningful XLM-R advantage in the clean, noisy and pooled analyses, and an inconclusive result on the real unseen documents. The synthetic result shows that XLM-R copes better with shifted wording and image noise on documents of this kind; it does not show that it is better on real HR documents, where the primary comparison stays inconclusive. The measured cost is unchanged, and the recommendation therefore stays as above: keep TF-IDF in production and, if robustness to new wording matters, evaluate XLM-R (or a cheaper hybrid) on a larger real, labelled, template-disjoint set before deciding.", align="j")


def hybrid_section(para, bullet, table, figure, D):
    H = D.get("hy")
    if not H: return
    sg = lambda x: f"{x:+.3f}"; ci = lambda a: f"{a[0]:+.3f} to {a[1]:+.3f}"; A = H["analyses"]; M = A["main_real_test_seeds0to4"]; M10 = A["main_real_test_seeds0to9"]; ce = H["cost_estimate"]
    para("4.5.15 Hybrid cascade: TF-IDF first, XLM-R only for uncertain documents", italic=True)
    para(f"Design (pre-registered in Addendum 4 before any hybrid prediction was computed): TF-IDF classifies every document; when its top probability is below a threshold the document is passed to XLM-R. One hybrid was formed per XLM-R seed, with the threshold chosen on the validation set only (real-only validation macro-F1; thresholds {', '.join(str(t) for t in M['tau_per_seed'])} for seeds 0-4). Nothing was retrained, calibrated or tuned on the test sets.", align="j")
    rows = [["Set", "Documents", "TF-IDF", "XLM-R", "Hybrid", "Sent to XLM-R", "Hybrid - TF-IDF (95% interval)", "Hybrid - XLM-R (95% interval)", "Verdict vs TF-IDF"]]
    for nm, k in (("Main real test", "main_real_test_seeds0to4"), ("Synthetic, clean", "unseen_synthetic_clean_seeds0to4"), ("Synthetic, noisy", "unseen_synthetic_noisy_seeds0to4"), ("Synthetic, long", "unseen_synthetic_long_seeds0to4"), ("Synthetic, all", "unseen_synthetic_pooled_seeds0to4"), ("Real Portuguese (2 classes)", "unseen_real_pt_seeds0to4")):
        a = A[k]; rows.append([nm, str(a["n_docs"]), f"{a['tfidf_macro_f1']:.3f}", f"{a['xlmr_macro_f1_mean']:.3f}", f"{a['hybrid_macro_f1_mean']:.3f}", f"{100 * a['routed_share_mean']:.0f}%", f"{sg(a['delta_vs_tfidf'])} ({ci(a['ci95_vs_tfidf'])})", f"{sg(a['delta_vs_xlmr'])} ({ci(a['ci95_vs_xlmr'])})", a["decision_vs_tfidf"].split(":")[0]])
    para("Table 21. Hybrid cascade (macro-F1; mean over the five pre-registered seeds; A+B/A = hybrid better than TF-IDF by the pre-registered rule, C = inconclusive).", italic=True, size=10); table(rows, widths=[1.1, 0.5, 0.45, 0.45, 0.45, 0.5, 1.25, 1.25, 0.5], fs=6.5)
    figure(FIG / "final_fig9_hybrid_curve.png", 4.9, "Fig. 22. Macro-F1 of the hybrid on the real test set as a function of the share of documents sent to XLM-R (descriptive only).")
    para(f"Verified. On the main real test set the hybrid scores {M['hybrid_macro_f1_mean']:.3f} against {M['tfidf_macro_f1']:.3f} for TF-IDF and {M['xlmr_macro_f1_mean']:.3f} for XLM-R alone, while sending only {100 * M['routed_share_mean']:.0f}% of documents to XLM-R; the difference from TF-IDF is {sg(M['delta_vs_tfidf'])} with hierarchical interval {ci(M['ci95_vs_tfidf'])}, whose lower end is essentially zero but not above it, so the pre-registered rule still returns inconclusive (ten seeds: {sg(M10['delta_vs_tfidf'])}, {ci(M10['ci95_vs_tfidf'])}). The difference from XLM-R alone is {sg(M['delta_vs_xlmr'])} ({ci(M['ci95_vs_xlmr'])}), i.e. the hybrid retained nearly all of XLM-R's observed gain on this set. On the synthetic set the hybrid is ahead of TF-IDF in every condition (clean, noisy, pooled: practically meaningful; long: not above the 0.02 threshold) and slightly behind XLM-R alone; on the real Portuguese documents it is inconclusive. Because the hybrid's gain is derived from the same XLM-R predictions, it carries the same test-set uncertainty: it is not independent confirmation of XLM-R's superiority.", align="j")
    para(f"Cost (estimate, not a measurement of the combined system). With {100 * ce['routed_share_main_test']:.0f}% of documents routed, the expected end-to-end CPU latency is {ce['hybrid_cpu_ms']:.1f} ms per document ({ce['tfidf_cpu_ms']:.2f} ms for TF-IDF plus {100 * ce['routed_share_main_test']:.0f}% of {ce['xlmr_cpu_ms']:.1f} ms), i.e. {ce['hybrid_over_tfidf_cpu']:.1f} times TF-IDF alone but {ce['hybrid_over_xlmr_cpu']:.2f} times XLM-R alone; with XLM-R on a GPU it is {ce['hybrid_gpu_xlmr_ms']:.1f} ms. The worst case (every document routed) is {ce['worst_case_all_routed_cpu_ms']:.1f} ms. The hybrid does not reduce the memory or disk footprint, because both models must be loaded (XLM-R's 25-fold size remains), and it adds a routing threshold to maintain; on the synthetic noisy set, where TF-IDF is unsure more often, {100 * A['unseen_synthetic_noisy_seeds0to4']['routed_share_mean']:.0f}% of documents were routed, so the saving depends on how uncertain TF-IDF is on the incoming documents.", align="j")
    para("Hypothesis (not tested). The routing curve in Fig. 22 suggests that most of XLM-R's gain on this test set sits in the few documents on which TF-IDF is uncertain, which is also where the small classes lie; this was not verified separately and the curve was not used to choose thresholds. Conclusion for the hybrid: it is the most cost-effective way to use XLM-R if XLM-R is eventually shown to be better, and it did not alter the primary verdict; it should be re-evaluated on the larger real test set recommended above before any deployment.", align="j")
